<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Auguste Rodin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1840">1840</a>–<a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as François Auguste René Rodin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Auguste Rodin was a French sculptor, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1840 into a working-class family, who is considered a founder of modern sculpture. Rejected three times by the École des Beaux Arts, he trained at the Petite École and in studios and workshops, and after a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1875, where he studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatello">Donatello</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>, made The Age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a>, so naturalistic that critics accused him of casting it from a living model. His works include The Gates of Hell, with the figure later known as The Thinker, The Kiss, The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">Burghers</a> of Calais and the Monument to Balzac. Camille Claudel was his student and lover; he married his companion Rose Beuret in 1917 and died that year at Meudon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/auguste-rodin.jpg" alt="Auguste Rodin" width="330" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Auguste_Rodin_fotografato_da_Nadar_nel_1891.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Degas’s Little Dancer of Fourteen Years, shown in 1881, struck some critics as overthrowing sculptural tradition as Impressionism had overthrown painting, and <b>Auguste Rodin</b> and Medardo Rosso have been called Impressionist sculptors.</blockquote><a href="/movement/impressionism">Impressionism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It has inspired artists for almost seven centuries: musicians from Franz Liszt to Hozier, whose album Unreal Unearth (2023) draws on it; <b>Auguste Rodin</b> in sculpture; and Timothy Schmalz, who made 100 sculptures, one for each canto, for the 700th anniversary of Dante's death.</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
